#### 6. Architectural and Empirical Recommendations for Thesis Defense

| Dimension / Criterion | Non-IID ($\alpha = 0.5$) Finding | Scientific Defense Recommendation |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Drift Variance Dynamics** | Expands by $1.86\text{x} - 3.67\text{x}$ relative to IID ($\alpha=1000$). | Highlight that closed-loop variance tracking ($\nu_t$) gives FDA direct real-time visibility into heterogeneity, whereas fixed baselines operate blindly. |
| **Controller Setpoint** | **$k = 0.5$ decisively outperforms $k = 1.0$**. | Defend the fundamental duality: $k=1.0$ is optimal for IID (overcoming low-variance premature braking), while $k=0.5$ is optimal for non-IID (damping superlinear variance growth and preventing representation collapse). |
| **Deep Compute Stability** | Fixed RoLoRA E20 collapses (MNLI: $73.18\% \to 64.30\%$). FDA Pure E1-40 reaches **74.77%** with zero collapse. | Position FDA as an **essential adaptive stabilizer** that makes deep local compute safe in heterogeneous edge networks. |
| **Milestone Acceleration** | Reaches R@72% on MNLI in **21 rounds (9.79 h)** vs. RoLoRA E10's **43 rounds (26.25 h)**. | Emphasize real-world cost savings: **2.68x faster wall-clock training** and **51.2% bandwidth reduction** for equivalent accuracy. |
| **Overall Verdict** | **FDA-RoLoRA is strictly superior to RoLoRA under non-IID conditions.** | Conclude that closed-loop adaptive local epoch scheduling is not merely a compute optimization, but a fundamental prerequisite for robust decentralized adaptation under data heterogeneity. |
